[70.252, "o", "\r\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\r\n  mlassure Assessment Report\r\n  Target: fraud-detection-v2 (fraud-detection-endpoint)\r\n  Run at: 2026-06-10T11:25:24.892Z\r\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\r\n\r\n  ✓ SI-6(1)      satisfied              conf:high    evidence:5\r\n  ✓ AC-6(9)      satisfied              conf:high    evidence:1\r\n      gap: No deny statements or permission boundaries were returned — if SCPs or permission boundaries exist at the account/OU level, they were not assessed here. Human review of any applicable Service Control Policies would provide additional assurance.\r\n  ✓ AU-12(3)     satisfied              conf:high    evidence:3\r\n "]
[0.000, "o", "     gap: No gaps identified. All three required evidence points (registry approval, CreateEndpoint, UpdateEndpoint) were available and temporally consistent.\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "      gap: Human attestation could further confirm that the mlops-deploy-svc automation enforces registry approval as a gate — this is implied by the evidence but not directly observable from logs alone.\r\n  ✓ SC-28        satisfied              conf:high    evidence:1\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  ? SA-10        insufficient-evidence  conf:high    evidence:0\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "      gap: Human attestation is required: documented evidence of named reviewer sign-off on model changes for 'fraud-detection-v2' before deployment to 'fraud-detection-endpoint'. This could include: (1) a change management ticket (e.g., Jira, ServiceNow) with an approver's name and timestamp, (2) a pull request or code review record showing a named reviewer approved the model update, (3) a signed model review/approval document or checklist, (4) a deployment authorization record referencing the specific model version. None of these artifacts are machine-retrievable from AWS and must be provided via human attestation or a linked external artifact.\r\n"]
